                Mist evaporative cooling system with natural ventilation in a semi-outdoor environment: Theoretical models, experiments, and thermal simulations
                Cruz-Salas, M.V., Puerto, E., Rojas, J., Barrios, G., Huelsz, G., Ramírez, G.

                    [URL]
                    BibTeX
                    @article{cruz2026,    author = "Cruz-Salas, M.V. and Puerto, E. and Rojas, J. and Barrios, G. and Huelsz, G. and Ramírez, G.",    title = "Mist evaporative cooling system with natural ventilation in a semi-outdoor environment: Theoretical models, experiments, and thermal simulations",    journal = "Energy and Buildings",    volume = "366",    pages = "117700",    year = "2026",    issn = "0378-7788",    doi = "https://doi.org/10.1016/j.enbuild.2026.117700",    url = "https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0378778826007607",    keywords = "Evaporative cooling, Mist, Natural ventilation, Semi-outdoor, Thermodynamic modeling, Energyplus",    abstract = "Semi-outdoor environments in climates with a hot and dry season can achieve comfortable air temperature using mist evaporative cooling systems (MECS). However, assessing their effect using building thermal simulation programs requires a physical model of the MECS operating in combination with natural ventilation in semi-outdoor environments (MECS\&NV) that can be implemented in such programs. This research presents, step by step, the development of a thermodynamic model for the MECS\&NV process based on psychrometric theory, which can be implemented in EnergyPlus using its Python plug-in system. The developed model was validated against experimental results obtained in a semi-outdoor cafeteria. For the cafeteria air temperature, the mean absolute error is 0.4 ∘C and the mean bias error is 0.1 ∘C. The cooling effect of the MECS on the cafeteria air temperature compared with the value it would have without the MECS has a maximum reduction of 2.9 ∘C and an average of 1.9 ∘C. The developed model can be used to perform future simulations and support decision-making regarding sizing and implementation of this type of evaporative cooling system in semi-outdoor spaces for low-energy buildings."}



